📘 Overview of STL in C++
What is STL?
- Definition: STL is a set of template classes and functions in C++ designed to handle data structures and algorithms in a generic way.
- Purpose: It allows programmers to reuse well-tested components instead of writing everything from scratch.


🔑 Components of STL
STL is broadly divided into three main categories:
1.Containers (Data Structures)
    - Store collections of objects.
    - Examples:
        - Sequence Containers: vector, list, deque
        - Associative Containers: set, map, multiset, multimap
        - Unordered Containers: unordered_set, unordered_map
    - Each container has its own strengths (e.g., vector for fast random access, map for key-value pairs).
2.Algorithms
    - Predefined functions to perform operations on containers.
    - Examples:
        - Searching (find, binary_search)
        - Sorting (sort, stable_sort)
        - Modifying (copy, remove, transform)
    - Work seamlessly with iterators, making them flexible across different containers.
3.Iterators
    - Act like pointers to traverse containers.
    - Types:
        - Input, Output, Forward, Bidirectional, Random Access
    - Example: vector<int>::iterator it;




What is a Vector?
- Vector = Resizable Array
    A vector stores elements of the same data type in contiguous memory locations, just like arrays.
- Dynamic Size
    Unlike arrays, vectors can expand or contract during runtime depending on the number of elements.
note :-  in vector if we push 1 in the size increase by one but the capacity is doubled
         (capacity is nothing but the limit of holding an element and the size is the number of the element the vector have )


#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    // Declare an empty vector of integers
    vector<int> v1;

    // Declare a vector with size 3, initialized with value 5
    vector<int> v2(3, 5);

    // Add elements dynamically
    v1.push_back(10);
    v1.push_back(20);

    // Access elements
    cout << v1[0] << endl;   // prints 10
    cout << v2.at(1) << endl; // prints 5

    return 0;
}

Build-in function for the vector container :-
    #include <iostream>
    #include <vector>
    #include <algorithm>
    using namespace std;

    int main() {
        vector<int> v = {10, 20, 30};
        

        // push_back(value) → Adds element at end
        // Return type: void (does not return anything)
        v.push_back(40);
        // v = {10, 20, 30, 40}

        // pop_back() → Removes last element
        // Return type: void (does not return anything)
        v.pop_back();
        // v = {10, 20, 30}

        // insert(pos, value) → Inserts element at given position      //O(n)
        // Return type: iterator (points to inserted element)
        v.insert(v.begin() + 1, 15);
        // v = {10, 15, 20, 30}

        // erase(pos) → Removes element at given position        //O(n)
        // Return type: iterator (points to next element after erased one)
        v.erase(v.begin() + 2);
        // v = {10, 15, 30}

        // clear() → Removes all elements
        // Return type: void
        v.clear();
        // v = {}

        // Reinitialize for next examples
        v = {5, 10, 15};

        // at(index) → Access element with bounds checking
        // Return type: reference to element
        cout << v.at(1) << endl; // Output: 10

        // front() → First element
        // back() → Last element
        // Return type: reference to element
        cout << v.front() << " " << v.back() << endl; // Output: 5 15

        // size() → Number of elements
        // capacity() → Allocated storage (may be >= size)
        // Return type: size_t
        cout << "Size: " << v.size() << ", Capacity: " << v.capacity() << endl;

        // resize(n, val) → Change size, fill new slots with val
        // Return type: void
        v.resize(5, 100);
        // v = {5, 10, 15, 100, 100}

        // reserve(n) → Reserve capacity for at least n elements
        // Return type: void
        v.reserve(20);
        // capacity >= 20

        // swap(other) → Swap contents with another vector
        // Return type: void
        vector<int> v2 = {1, 2, 3};
        v.swap(v2);
        // v = {1, 2, 3}

        // emplace(pos, val) → Construct element in place at pos
        // Return type: iterator
        v.emplace(v.begin() + 1, 99);
        // v = {1, 99, 2, 3}

        // emplace_back(val) → Construct element in place at end
        // Return type: reference to new element
        v.emplace_back(77);
        // v = {1, 99, 2, 3, 77}

        // data() → Pointer to underlying array
        // Return type: T* (raw pointer)
        int* arr = v.data();
        cout << "First element via data(): " << arr[0] << endl; // Output: 1

        return 0;
    }

  ✅ Key Notes on Return Types
  - push_back, pop_back, clear, resize, reserve, swap → void (no return).
  - insert, erase, emplace → return iterator (useful for chaining).
  - emplace_back → returns reference to the newly added element.
  - at, front, back, data → return reference/pointer to element(s).
  - size, capacity, empty → return size_t / bool.


